[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the app pick which environment's API endpoint to use at runtime? → Hostname detection (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Hostname detection it is. Second question — and this one's about the module boundary itself, since the browser side
  has no module system today.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets at the settings object. Both real options are build-free; they differ in what the boundary
  looks like and whether opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m straight off disk still works.

  - [1mGlobal namespace[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m is a second classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and assigns one object (e.g.
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m). Nothing else changes, and [38;5;153mfile://[39m still works. Cost: it's a global, load order is load-bearing
    and silent if you get it wrong, and it isn't a real module despite the name.
  - [1mNative ES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does [38;5;153mimport { settings } from [39m
    [38;5;153m'./settings.js'[39m. A genuine boundary with explicit dependencies and no globals, no toolchain needed. Cost: ES modules
    are blocked by CORS on [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so from then on the page must be opened through a local server.

  Neither is expensive to reverse later; this is mostly about whether you care about the [38;5;153mfile://[39m workflow.

  I'd go with [1mnative ES modules[0m. It's the actual module the request asks for rather than a global wearing a module's
  name, it makes [38;5;153mapp.js[39m's dependency explicit, and it costs nothing but a [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m when you want to look
  at the page.

  Worth noting for either choice: I'd leave [38;5;153msrc/[39m (CommonJS, Node-side) alone entirely — it never touches the API
  endpoint, and dragging it in would be scope creep.
